Category: Malware Development / Kernel Rootkit / Windows Internals / DKOM

# Scenario
Blue pill: stay in user mode, trust the documented APIs, and never question what lies beneath. Red pill: drop into kernel mode and see how deep the rabbit hole goes. The challenge involved developing a Windows kernel-mode rootkit using Direct Kernel Object Manipulation (DKOM) to hide a running process from user-space enumeration, building it as a signed driver using the Windows Driver Kit, and uploading the compiled payload to a target machine via a web interface.

